mprove (3) we use the Erd\\\\H{o}s–Szekeres theorem.  Any permutation of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ contains a monotone (increasing or decreasing) subsequence of length $m=\\\\lceil\\\\sqrt n\\\\rceil$.  Without loss of generality assume there are indices\\n\\\\[1\\\\le i_1<i_2<\\\\dots<i_m\\\\le n\\\\quad\\\\text{with}\\\\quad p(i_1)<p(i_2)<\\\\dots<p(i_m).\\\\]\\nLet $R=\\\\{i_1,\\\\dots,i_m\\\\}$ and $C=\\\\{p(i_1),\\\\dots,p(i_m)\\\\}$.  The $m\\\\times m$ submatrix $A[R,C]$ is exactly the complement of a permutation matrix (the identity after renumbering).  The following lemma gives a sharp bound for such a submatrix.\\n\\n**Lemma 1.**  Let $B$ be the $m\\\\times m$ $0$–$1$ matrix that has a single $0$ in each row and each column (i.e. the complement of a permutation matrix).  Any partition of the $1$‑entries of $B$ into rectangles uses at least $2m-3$ rectangles.\\n\\n*Proof of Lemma 1.*  Number the rows and columns $1,\\\\dots,m$ so that the $0$‑entries lie on the main diagonal.  For $1\\\\le r<m$ the cell $(r,r+1)$ lies strictly above the diagonal, and for $1< c\\\\le m$ the cell $(c,c-1)$ lies strictly below the diagonal.  Any rectangle that contains a cell above the diagonal cannot contain a cell below the diagonal, because otherwise it would also contain a diagonal $0$‑cell, contradicting (1).  Hence each rectangle can cover cells of at most one of the two families\\n\\\\[U=\\\\{(r,r+1)\\\\mid1\\\\le r<m\\\\},\\\\qquad D=\\\\{(c,c-1)\\\\mid1<c\\\\le m\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nBoth $U$ and $D$ consist of $m-1$ pairwise disjoint cells, so at least $m-1$ rectangles are needed for $U$ and at least $m-1$ for $D$.  The cell $(1,m)$ (top‑right corner) belongs to neither $U$ nor $D$; it must be covered by some rectangle, and that rectangle can be used for at most one of the two families.  Consequently the total number of rectangles is at least\\n\\\\[(m-1)+(m-1)+1=2m-1.\\\\]\\nA slightly sharper counting observes that the two corner cells $(1,1)$ and $(m,m)$ are $0$‑entries