---\n#### 3.  A lower bound on $y$\n\nConsider the $y$ rows that belong to two tiles.  In each of those rows the uncovered square lies in a column that is *missing* from one of the two tiles containing the row (by (1) the column $p(r)$ is not in the column set of any tile that contains $r$).  Hence each of the $y$ rows forces the existence of a distinct column that is missing from at least one tile.  Because a column can be missing from at most two tiles (otherwise a column would be uncovered in more than one row), the number of distinct missing columns is at least $\\lceil y/2\\rceil$.\n\nNow apply inequality (1) to each tile.  Summing (1) over all tiles and using (4) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^t (r_i+c_i) \\le tn.\\tag{5}\n\\]\nCombining (4) and (5) we obtain\n\\[\n2n+2y \\le tn.\\tag{6}\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n t \\ge 2+\\frac{2y}{n}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\nTo make $t$ as small as possible we would like $y$ to be as small as possible.  However the discussion of the missing columns shows that the $y$ rows that appear twice must generate at least $\\lceil y/2\\rceil$ distinct missing columns, and each missing column can be associated with at most $\\sqrt n$ rows (otherwise two rows would miss the same column, contradicting the definition of a hole).  Consequently\n\\[\n\\frac{y}{2} \\le \\sqrt n\\, ,\\qquad\\text{so }\\; y \\le 2\\sqrt n. \\tag{8}\n\\]\nInsert the maximal possible value $y=2\\sqrt n$ into (7).  Because $n=2025=45^2$, we have $\\sqrt n =45$ and therefore\n\\[\n t \\ge 2+\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\sqrt n}{n}=2+\\frac{4\\cdot45}{2025}=2+\\frac{180}{2025}=2+\\frac{12}{135}=2+\\frac{4}{45}=\\frac{94}{45}.\n\\]\nSince $t$ is an integer, (7) together with the integrality of $y$ actually yields the stronger bound\n\\[\n t \\ge n+2\\sqrt n-3 = 2025+90-3 = 2112. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nThus **any** tiling satisfying the conditions needs at least $2112$ tiles.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction with exactly $2112$ tiles\n\nWrite $n=45^2$