ion \\(\\pi\\) so that the set \\(H\\) is a *grid permutation*: split the rows and columns into \\(m\\) blocks of size \\(m\\) and place the holes on the block\u2011diagonal, i.e.\n\\[(i,\\pi(i))\\in\\bigl\\{( (a-1)m+b,\\;(b-1)m+a)\\mid 1\\le a,b\\le m\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\nIn this arrangement each block contains exactly one hole, situated on the main diagonal of the block.\nNow look at a tile \\(R\\).  Because of condition (1), the set \\(R_{row}\\) cannot contain two rows belonging to the same block while \\(R_{col}\\) contains the corresponding column of the hole, otherwise the hole would lie inside \\(R\\).  Consequently **the rows of a tile intersect at most two different row\u2011blocks**, and the same holds for its columns.\nHence a tile can be adjacent to holes that lie in at most two distinct blocks.  Moreover, within a single block a tile can be adjacent to at most two holes (the two corners of the block that are on the boundary of the tile).  Therefore a tile can be adjacent to at most \\(2+2=4\\) holes.\nSumming over all tiles we now have\n\\[4t\\ge n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\ge \\frac n4.\\]\nAgain this is not sufficient.  To reach the exact bound we refine the argument by counting *block\u2011corner incidences*.\n\nFor each block \\(B_{ab}\\) (the \\(a\\)-th row\u2011block and \\(b\\)-th column\u2011block) consider the four grid points that are the corners of the block.  A tile can contain at most one of these four corners, because if it contained two opposite corners it would also contain the whole block and thus the hole inside it \u2013 which is forbidden.  Hence each tile contributes at most one corner\u2011incidence per block.\nSince there are \\(m^2\\) blocks, the total number of corner\u2011incidences over all tiles is at most \\(t\\cdot m^2\\).\nOn the other hand, every block must have its four corners covered by tiles (otherwise some unit square of the block would remain uncovered).  Thus the total number of required corner\u2011incidences is 