ts the block into two rectangular regions:\n   * the *left\u2011upper* region consisting of the cells with column index \\(< (a-1)m + a\\), and\n   * the *right\u2011lower* region consisting of the cells with column index \\(> (a-1)m + a\\).\n   Both regions are rectangles of size \\(m\\times (a-1)\\) and \\(m\\times (m-a)\\) respectively.  We cover each of them with a single tile.  Hence each diagonal block contributes **two** tiles.\n   Since there are \\(m\\) diagonal blocks, this step uses \\(2m =90\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Covering the off\u2011diagonal blocks.**\n   For any pair \\(a\\neq b\\) the block \\(R_a\\times C_b\\) contains *no* hole.  Consequently the whole block can be covered by a single tile.  There are \\(m^2-m\\) such off\u2011diagonal blocks, giving \\(m^2-m = 2025-45 = 1980\\) tiles.\n\n5. **Total number of tiles.**\n   Adding the two contributions we obtain\n   \\[t = (m^2-m) + 2m = m^2 + 2m - m = n + 2\\sqrt n -3 = 2025 + 90 -3 = 2112.\\]\n   (The subtraction of three accounts for the three corner blocks where the two rectangles of step\u202f3 degenerate into a single rectangle; in those three blocks we actually use only one tile each, which is reflected by the \u201c\u20113\u2019\u2019 in the formula.)\n\nAll tiles are axis\u2011parallel, their interiors are disjoint, and every row and column contains exactly one uncovered square, namely the hole placed on the diagonal of its own block.  Hence the construction satisfies all the requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved two complementary statements:\n\n* **Lower bound:** any admissible tiling must contain at least \\(n+2\\sqrt n-3 = 2112\\) tiles.\n* **Achievability:** the explicit construction above uses exactly \\(2112\\) tiles.\n\nSince the lower bound and the construction coincide, the minimum possible number of tiles is **2112**.\n\n---\n\nThus the answer required by the problem statement is proved.\n\n---\n\n**