e column of the hole in row i. In block representation, each row block a contains rows (a-1)k+1 to a k. The hole in each row i is at column pi(i). In the block arrangement, each row block a has exactly one hole in column block a (the diagonal block). So any rectangle that includes rows from block a and columns from block b (b != a) will not contain any hole because the holes in those rows are in column block a, not b. So we can combine multiple off-diagonal blocks in same row block a across multiple column blocks b != a, as long as we avoid column block a. Similarly, we can combine across multiple row blocks for a fixed column block b != a. So we can potentially cover many off-diagonal blocks with fewer rectangles by merging them into larger rectangles that avoid the diagonal blocks.\n\nFor example, consider all off-diagonal blocks in rows block a (a fixed) and columns block b != a. The union of those blocks is rows a (k rows) and columns all blocks except a (k-1 blocks, each of width k). This region is a rectangle of size k rows by (k*(k-1)) columns, i.e., k x (k^2 - k). Does this rectangle contain any holes? The holes in rows a are at column block a (the diagonal block). Since we exclude column block a, the rectangle does not contain any holes. So we can cover all off-diagonal blocks in row block a with a single rectangle! Similarly, we can cover all off-diagonal blocks in column block b with a single rectangle. But careful: If we cover all off-diagonal blocks in row block a with one rectangle, we would also cover the off-diagonal blocks in column block a? Actually the rectangle covering rows a and all columns except a includes all off-diagonal blocks in row a. For each other row block a', the rectangle does not include those rows, so not covering those off-diagonal blocks. So we would need one rectangle per row block (except maybe we can combine across row blocks? Let's see). Similarly, we could cover all off-diagonal blocks in column block b with a rectangle cov