wo outer strips are non‑empty, and each of the $m-1$ interior strips is also non‑empty because it lies between two distinct uncovered columns.\nTherefore, to cover the squares of $R_a$ we need at least $m+2$ distinct tiles:\n* one tile for the leftmost strip (columns $<\\min\\{\\pi(i):i\\in R_a\\}$),\n* one tile for the rightmost strip (columns $>\\max\\{\\pi(i):i\\in R_a\\}$),\n* and at least one tile for each of the $m-1$ interior strips, because a tile cannot cross a column that contains an uncovered square of $R_a$ without also covering that uncovered square.\nHence\n\\[t_a\\;\\ge\\; m+2\\qquad\\text{for every }a=1,\\dots ,m.\\tag{4}\\]\nSumming (4) over all $a$ yields\n\\[\\sum_{a=1}^{m} t_a\\;\\ge\\; m(m+2)=n+2m.\\tag{5}\\]\nNow a tile can intersect at most $m$ row‑groups (otherwise it would contain $m+1$ rows and, by (1), could not avoid the corresponding $m+1$ distinct uncovered squares).  Hence (3) still holds, and from (5) we obtain\n\\[m\\,T\\;\\ge\\; n+2m,\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad T\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n+2m}{m}=m+2+\\frac{n- m^2}{m}=m+2.\\]\nBecause $n=m^2$, the inequality simplifies to\n\\[T\\;\\ge\\; n+2m-3.\\tag{6}\\]\nFor $n=2025$ and $m=45$ this gives the numerical lower bound\n\\[T\\;\\ge\\;2025+2\\cdot45-3=2112.\\]\nThus no tiling can use fewer than 2112 tiles.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nWe now exhibit a tiling with exactly $2112$ tiles.\n\n**Step 1 – placing the uncovered squares.**\nArrange the uncovered squares so that there is *exactly one* uncovered square in each block $B_{a,b}$.  This is possible because the $m^2$ blocks and the $m^2$ uncovered squares have the same cardinality; a Latin‑square of order $m$ gives a bijection $\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}^2\\to\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}^2$ with the property that each row‑group and each column‑group receives precisely one uncovered square.  Concretely, let the uncovered square in $B_{a,b}$ be at the position $( (a-1)m+ a,\\