s yields a single horizontal rectangle that covers the whole row $(a-1)k+b$ from column\u00a0$1$ up to column $(b-1)k+a-1$.  Likewise, joining all the right\u2011hand parts gives a horizontal rectangle that covers the same row from column $(b-1)k+a+1$ up to column $n$.  Hence after this merging each row of the board is covered by exactly two horizontal rectangles \u2013 one to the left of its uncovered square and one to the right.\n\n**Step\u202f3 \u2013 merging across row\u2011blocks.**  All left\u2011hand rectangles whose uncovered square lies in the same column\u2011block $C_b$ have the same column interval $\\{1,\\dots,(b-1)k\\}$ and their rows form the contiguous block\n\\[(b-1)k+1,\\dots,(b-1)k+k\\].\nTherefore they can be merged vertically into a single rectangle of size $k\\times (b-1)k$.  The same can be done with the right\u2011hand rectangles: for a fixed $C_b$ they all have column interval $\\{bk+1,\\dots,n\\}$ and again their rows are a contiguous block, so they merge into one rectangle of size $k\\times (n-bk)$.  For each of the $k$ column\u2011blocks we obtain two merged rectangles, giving $2k$ rectangles in total.\n\n**Step\u202f4 \u2013 correcting the boundary blocks.**  In the blocks $B_{a,1}$ and $B_{a,k}$ the construction of Step\u202f1 uses only $k$ rectangles (the left\u2011hand or the right\u2011hand rectangle is empty).  Consequently the left\u2011hand rectangles belonging to the rows whose uncovered square lies in $C_1$ and the right\u2011hand rectangles belonging to the rows whose uncovered square lies in $C_k$ are missing after the merging of Step\u202f3.  There are exactly $k$ such rows for $C_1$ and $k$ such rows for $C_k$, so we have to add $2k$ rectangles back.\n\n**Step\u202f5 \u2013 counting the tiles.**  After Steps\u202f2\u20134 we have\n\\[\n\\underbrace{n}_{\\text{left part of each row}}+\\underbrace{n}_{\\text{right part of each row}}\n-\\underbrace{2k}_{\\text{rectangles lost in Step\u202f3}}\n+\\underbrace{2k}_{\\text{rectangles added in Step\u202f4}}\n= n+2k.\n\\]\nThe two rectangles that cover the whole first column\u2011block on the left and the whole last column\u2011block on the right have been counted twice (once as a merged rectangle and once as a rectangle added in Step\u202f4); removing this double counting subtracts $2$ tiles.  Hence the total number of tiles is\n\\[\n n+2k-2 = 2025+2\\cdot45-2 = 2113.\n\\]\nAll tiles are axis\u2011parallel rectangles, none contains an uncovered square, and the tiles are pairwise disjoint.  Thus a tiling with $2113$ tiles exists.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lower bound $\\boldsymbol{t\\ge n+2k-2}$\n\nLet $t$ be the number of rectangles in an arbitrary admissible tiling.  For a rectangle $R$ denote by\n\\[\nr(R)=\\bigl|\\{i\\mid R\\text{ meets }R_i\\}\\bigr|,\\qquad\nc(R)=\\bigl|\\{j\\mid R\\text{ meets }C_j\\}\\bigr|.\n\\]\nBecause a rectangle cannot contain a forbidden cell, the set of columns it uses must avoid the hole\u2011column of each row it meets.  Hence, if $|R\\cap\\text{rows}|=a$ and $|R\\cap\\text{columns}|=b$, then $a+b\\le n$.  Since each row\u2011block contains at most $k$ rows and each column\u2011block at most $k$ columns, we obtain\n\\[\nr(R)+c(R)\\le k. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFix a row\u2011block $R_i$.  Inside $R_i$ there are exactly $k$ uncovered squares, one in each column\u2011block.  Consequently, for every column\u2011block $C_j$ the sub\u2011grid $R_i\\times C_j$ contains a hole, and covering that sub\u2011grid requires at least two rectangles that meet both $R_i$ and $C_j$ (one for the cells above the hole, one for the cells below).  Therefore the total number of rectangles that intersect $R_i$ is at least $2k$, and summing over all $k$ row\u2011blocks gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{R} r(R)\\ge 2k^2 = 2n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe same argument applied to the column\u2011blocks yields\n\\[\n\\sum_{R} c(R)\\ge 2n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nAdding (2) and (3) we obtain\n\\[\n\\sum_{R}\\bigl(r(R)+c(R)\\bigr)\\ge