                "reasoning_content": " need to evaluate the provided solution for the problem: \"Consider a 2025×2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nThe solution claims the answer is 2113. It provides a construction achieving 2113 tiles and a lower bound proving that at least 2113 tiles are needed. So answer is correct.\n\nWe need to apply hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, identify problem type: It's an optimization problem: \"minimum number of tiles\". So it's a MIN problem. According to Level 1: For optimization problems, we do NOT check correctness at this level; only check for arithmetic errors or constraint violations. Then proceed to Level 1.5.\n\nWe need to check if any arithmetic errors or constraints violation in the solution's claim. The solution defines n=2025, k=√n=45. Indeed 45^2 = 2025. Good.\n\nConstruction: Partition into k×k blocks. Place uncovered squares as described. That ensures each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. The construction seems plausible. The solution then constructs a tiling using rectangles covering all other squares (except uncovered). The counting yields n+2k-2 = 2025+90-2 = 2113. That seems consistent.\n\nWe need to verify if any arithmetic errors: The counting steps: Step 5: they claim after merging they have n left parts + n right parts - 2k lost + 2k added = n+2k. Then they subtract double counting of two rectangles (first column-block left and last column-block right) subtract 2, giving n+2k-2. That seems correct.\n\nCheck lower bound: They define r(R) and c(R) as number of row-blocks and column-blocks intersected. They claim r(R)+c(R) ≤ k (Equation (1)