                    "reasoning": " need to verify the provided solution for the problem: \"Consider a 2025×2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nThe solution claims the minimum number is 2113, with construction achieving n+2k-2 where n=2025, k=45 (since sqrt(2025)=45). They give a construction and a lower bound argument.\n\nWe need to evaluate according to the hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, identify if this is an optimization problem: Yes, it's a minimization problem: \"minimum number of tiles\". So it's an optimization problem (MIN). According to Level 1, for optimization problems we do not check answer correctness at this level (since we don't have ground truth). We only check for obvious errors: arithmetic mistakes, constraint violations. Then proceed to Level 1.5.\n\nThus we must check if there are any obvious errors: The solution uses n=2025, k=45. They propose a construction with n+2k-2 = 2025 + 90 - 2 = 2113 tiles. We need to check if the construction indeed satisfies constraints: each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square, each tile is a rectangle aligned with grid lines, tiles are disjoint (no overlapping), each unit square covered at most once, and each tile does not contain any uncovered square. Also each uncovered square is exactly one per row and column.\n\nWe need to verify if there are any obvious errors: e.g., miscounting, missing tiles, overlapping, uncovered squares not placed correctly, etc. Also check if the lower bound argument is valid.\n\nWe must not re-prove the solution, but evaluate the reasoning and check for errors.\n\nWe need to follow the hierarchical decision tree:\n\nLevel 1: For optimization problem, check for arithmetic errors and constraint violations.\n\nPotential arithmetic errors: They compute n+2k-2 = 2025+90-2 = 2113. That's correct.\n\nCheck the construction: They partition the board into k=45 row blocks and column blocks each of size 45x45. In each block B_{a,b} they place exactly one uncovered unit square at ((a-1)k+b, (b-1)k+a). Let's verify that this indeed gives exactly one uncovered per row and column.\n\nRows: For fixed a, the uncovered squares in row-block R_a have row indices (a-1)k+b for b=1..k, which are distinct and cover each row in that block exactly once. So each row in the whole board has exactly one uncovered square.\n\nColumns: For fixed b, the column indices are (b-1)k+a for a=1..k, which are distinct and cover each column in column-block C_b exactly once. So each column has exactly one uncovered square.\n\nThus the uncovered squares are placed correctly.\n\nNow the construction of tiles: They claim each block B_{a,b} (size k×k) with a hole at relative position (b,a) can be tiled by k+1 rectangles: all rows except row b are covered by a full horizontal rectangle of size 1×k (so k-1 rectangles). In row b, they take two horizontal rectangles: left part of size 1×(a-1) and right part of size 1×(k-a). So total k+1 rectangles (or k if a=1 or a=k because one of the two small rectangles is empty). That's a valid tiling of each block individually.\n\nThen they merge left-hand parts across blocks in a row-block, forming a single horizontal rectangle per row covering left side of each uncovered square. Similarly merging right-hand parts across blocks yields a horizontal rectangle covering right side of each uncovered square. So each row is covered by exactly two horizontal rectangles: left and right of the uncovered square.\n\nThen they merge vertically across row-blocks for each column-block: left-hand rectangles belonging to rows whose uncovered squares lie in column-block C_b have same column inte