                    "reasoning_content": "We need to evaluate the provided solution for the problem: \"Consider a 2025×2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nThe solution claims answer 2113.\n\nWe need to follow hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, identify problem type: It's an optimization problem: \"minimum number of tiles\". So it's a MINIMIZATION problem. According to Level 1: For optimization problems, we do NOT check correctness at this level (no ground truth). We only check for obvious errors: arithmetic mistakes, constraint violations. Then we must proceed to Level 1.5 (optimality check). So we cannot simply say answer is correct or wrong. We need to check if there are any arithmetic errors or constraint violations in the solution.\n\nGiven the solution: They propose a lower bound using fooling set argument and an explicit construction achieving that bound. We need to see if there are any arithmetic mistakes. The lower bound: n = 2025, k = sqrt(n) = 45. They compute n + 2k - 2 = 2025 + 90 - 2 = 2113. That's arithmetic correct.\n\nCheck the construction: They partition board into k×k blocks (45×45). They place holes at positions ((i-1)k + j, (j-1)k + i) for i,j from 1 to k. That is a transversal of blocks. They claim each block can be tiled with k+1 rectangles (or k if hole at leftmost or rightmost column). Then they merge left-hand pieces across column groups to form vertical rectangles, similarly for right-hand pieces. Then they add missing edge pieces as 2k separate 1×k rectangles. Then they count total rectangles: n + 2k - 2 = 2113. They claim there is double counting of two rectangles covering whole fi